WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.020
The central habit in memory-constrained secure programming is to make limits explicit.

2
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:17.320
Define the memory budget early: RAM, stack, heap, static storage, fixed buffers, pools, scratch space, persistent records, and any special memory regions.

3
00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.460
Tie those limits to real product assumptions.

4
00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:24.420
Bound the code paths that consume memory.

5
00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.920
What is the maximum input size?

6
00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:29.260
What stack depth is possible?

7
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.440
Which allocations are allowed?

8
00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:33.540
What happens when a buffer fills?

9
00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.520
Can large input be streamed instead of stored?

10
00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:39.660
Which parser state is retained across chunks?

11
00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:44.240
These questions turn vague constraints into reviewable contracts.

12
00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.840
Handle low-memory behavior predictably.

13
00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:54.780
Allocation failure, full buffers, oversized input, and limited diagnostic space should lead to safe, documented results.

14
00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:03.200
Preserve safe state, clean up owned resources, avoid partial updates, bound retries, and report errors in a way callers can handle.

15
00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:06.120
Protect sensitive data even when memory is tight.

16
00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:12.060
Minimize copies of keys, credentials, tokens, identifiers, and intermediate plaintext.

17
00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:17.280
Review reused buffers, logs, crash records, and diagnostics.

18
00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:23.380
Clear sensitive buffers where supported and appropriate, and keep lifetimes as short as practical.

19
00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:28.060
Finally, measure and test worst-case paths before release.

20
00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:38.800
Use stack measurements, allocation tracking, boundary tests, malformed input tests, long-duration runs, static analysis, compiler warnings, and target-like configurations.

21
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:43.620
Secure constrained C is not one allocator, one wrapper, or one rule.

22
00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:51.480
It is a repeatable practice of explicit limits, bounded behavior, safe failure, and evidence from realistic tests.
